  "description": "Worked estimate of annual net pay after federal and provincial income tax and employee payroll contributions, for a single employee with only employment income, at three gross salaries, in each province and territory.",
  "attributes": {
    "method": "Gross salary minus federal tax, provincial/territorial tax (including Ontario surtax and Health Premium) and employee CPP/QPP, CPP2/QPP2, EI and QPIP. Taxable income = salary minus the deductible enhanced CPP/QPP portions. Federal credits at 14%: basic personal amount, Canada employment amount, base CPP/QPP portion, EI (and QPIP in Quebec). Provincial credits at each jurisdiction's lowest rate: basic personal amount, base CPP and EI. Quebec: federal tax reduced by the 16.5% abatement; Quebec tax = brackets minus 14% x $18,952 only.",
    "assumptions": [
      "Single, no dependants, under 65, resident in the province on 31 December 2026 for the full year",
      "All income is employment income from one employer; no RRSP, union dues, childcare, moving, medical or other credits",
      "Manitoba thresholds $47,000/$100,000 (see disputed note in nat-mb-income-and-sales-tax-2026)",
      "Quebec: the deduction for workers (maximum $1,450), any Quebec credit for QPP/EI/QPIP contributions, the Health Services Fund contribution and any public drug-plan premium are NOT modelled; Quebec net pay is therefore approximate (the omitted deduction for workers alone would add up to roughly $200–$370 of net pay)",
      "Low-income reductions (e.g. BC, Ontario) are nil at these salaries and are not modelled",
      "Employer-provided benefits and their taxable value ignored"
    ],
    "salaries": [
      60000,
      100000,
      150000
    ],
